@@ -21,6 +21,18 @@ A few defaults differ between the CUDA and ROCm builds. They are collected in
 
 ## Attention
 
+### `EXL3_GRAPHS` (default: `1` on CUDA, `0` on ROCm)
+
+Whether the decode paths capture their kernel sequences into device graphs and replay them. With
+`0` every graphed site (attention, MLA, GDN, the MLP and block-sparse decode kernels) runs the same
+C++ launch sequence eagerly on each call, kernels and order unchanged, so the switch changes only
+host submission. On CUDA replay is a few percent faster at decode. On ROCm the HIP runtime launches a
+graph node no faster than a plain kernel, the measured difference is within noise, and every
+instantiated graph exec reserves a 2 MB device-side kernel-argument pool that grows with each
+parameter update until the exec is destroyed (an open HIP runtime issue); a speculative-decoding
+server holds hundreds of execs, so disabling graphs there also frees over a gigabyte of VRAM.
+Set `EXL3_GRAPHS=1` on ROCm to test the graph path.
+
 ### `EXL3_BC_ATTN` (default: `1`)
 
 Graph-captured C++ decode attention. For decode steps (bsz ≤ 8, q_len ≤ 16) the whole attention
@@ -37,12 +49,51 @@ span-heads head norms, non-EXL3 projections, compander-enabled quant cache, ...)
 the regular dispatch path by design. Unexpected errors while building the path are raised, not
 swallowed. Set to `0` to disable the path entirely.
 
+### `EXL3_GRAPH_REINST` (ROCm; default: `100000`)
+
+HIP keeps a fixed pool of kernel-argument space per instantiated graph and does not reclaim it
+on `hipGraphExecKernelNodeSetParams`: after about 1.6M parameter updates (around an hour of
+serving with graph-captured decode) the next update segfaults inside libamdhip64. A graph is
+therefore re-instantiated once it has taken this many node parameter updates, and its patched
+nodes are set again from their current values. `0` disables. No effect on CUDA builds.
+
 ### `EXL3_BC_ATTN_TRACE` (default: `0`)
 
 Print one line per attention module/cache-layer pair when the graph-captured decode path is
 built or declined (module key, device). Activation check for A/B tests: a benchmark comparing
 `EXL3_BC_ATTN` settings is only meaningful if the enabled run actually built the path.
 
+### `EXL3_DEC_GROUP` (default: `0`)
+
+Split-decode attention, graph-captured and eager: a program normally takes 16 query rows
+(q_len x GQA heads), so a multi-token verify (q_len 8, group of 6) spreads one GQA group over
+several programs, each re-reading and re-dequantizing the same K/V tiles. `1` gives each
+program the whole group (q_len x next_pow2(group) rows, at least 16) and, unless
+`EXL3_DEC_SPLIT_MULT` is set, aims for 8 (single-token) or 4 (multi-token) programs per CU
+instead of 2. Tuned on gfx1201.
+
+### `EXL3_DEC_BLOCK_H` (default: unset)
+
+GQA heads per split-decode program while `EXL3_DEC_GROUP` is off; a power of two.
+
+### `EXL3_DEC_SPLIT_MULT` (default: unset)
+
+Programs per CU (a float) the split-decode split count aims for.
+
+### `EXL3_DEC_WARPS`, `EXL3_DEC_STAGES`, `EXL3_DEC_BLOCK_N` (default: unset)
+
+Override the split-decode kernel's warps, pipeline stages and kv tile (powers of two; stages
+non-negative) over the per-backend / per-architecture defaults (`attn_decode_config` in
+`exllamav3/util/backend.py`). Each applies on its own, in the eager launch and the
+graph-captured build alike; explicit arguments to `paged_attn_triton_decode` still win. A set
+`EXL3_DEC_BLOCK_N` replaces the shared-memory ladder with that one tile.
+
+### `EXL3_DEC_Q4W` (default: `0`)
+
+Word-sliced split-decode kernel for 4-bit K and V caches (head_dim a power of two, at least 128):
+each tile's packed words are loaded once and the dots split into 8 per-nibble partial dots,
+instead of unpacking through an LDS shuffle. 1.2-1.3x the packed kernel on gfx1201 at q_len 4-8.
+
 ### `EXL3_BC_DSA` (default: `1`)
 
 DeepSeek-V4 counterpart of `EXL3_BC_ATTN`: for decode steps (bsz 1, q_len ≤ 16) the whole DSA
@@ -106,6 +157,29 @@ Gated-delta-net (Qwen3-Next/3.5, KDA in GLM-5.3/Kimi Linear) counterpart of `EXL
 the decode step of a linear-attention layer runs as one graph-captured C++ call. Set to `0` to
 force the torch path, for A/B testing.
 
+### `EXL3_GDN_REPLAY` (default: `0`)
+
+Accepted-input replay for gated-delta-net layers under speculative decoding. Set to `1` and the
+cache keeps no per-draft-token recurrent-state history (about 0.15 GB per draft token on a
+48-layer model): the verify pass reads the committed state without writing it, and the rewind
+replays only the accepted prefix of the recorded verify inputs. The resulting state is bit-identical
+to the history path. Mamba2 layers keep their history.
+
+### `EXL3_BC_MLP_MAX_ROWS` (default: `32`)
+
+Most rows (bsz x q_len) the graphed dense GatedMLP path (`BC_GatedMLP`) takes; larger inputs run
+the eager path. The limit is 32 (`MLP_MAX_ROWS`, which also sizes the shared static scratch), so a
+batched speculative verify up to 4 x 8 rows stays graphed; `8` restores the previous limit.
+Read at import.
+
+### `EXL3_BC_GDN_REPLAY` (default: `1`)
+
+With `EXL3_GDN_REPLAY=1`, the verify pass also runs as the graph-captured C++ call. Its conv
+input, post-conv q/k/v, beta and g stay in per-layer buffers (about 0.33 MB per layer for an
+8-row verify; shapes share power-of-two row buckets), and two launches per rewind (recurrent state, conv window) commit the accepted
+prefix of every layer (`batched_gdn_replay`). Set to `0` to verify on the torch path and replay layer by layer, for
+A/B testing.
+
 ### `EXL3_GDN_SUB_CHUNK` (default: `2048`)
 
 Prefill of a gated-delta-net / KDA layer runs the fla chunked scan over consecutive sub-ranges
@@ -234,6 +308,28 @@ the path wherever its hard constraints allow (testing).
 Weight-extraction strategy inside the fp16 GEMV kernel: `-1` picks per bitrate (default), `0`
 forces shuffle extraction, `1` forces shared-memory staging. Testing only.
 
+### `EXL3_GEMV_WMMA` (ROCm gfx1200 / gfx1201; default: `0`)
+
+Multi-row EXL3 GEMV (2-32 rows, e.g. a speculative verify, or a batched one at bsz 4 x 8 rows) on
+the gfx12 WMMA units: each 16x16 trellis tile is decoded once and one `v_wmma_f32_16x16x16_f16`
+per 16 rows takes them all, instead of one fdot2 chain per row. Covers integer bitrates in the single-matrix
+GEMV and the unweighted single-token multi-matrix GEMV; everything else keeps the fdot2 kernels
+(2-8 rows) or the cooperative GEMM (9 rows and up). Devices other than
+gfx1200 / gfx1201 (gfx11 included) also keep them whatever the setting: eligibility is checked per
+device at runtime. Results match the fdot2 path to fp32 rounding, not bit for bit. Read on every
+call, unlike most C++-side variables, so tests can toggle it.
+
+### `EXL3_GEMV_MAX_M` (ROCm; default: `32`)
+
+Largest row count the RDNA multi-row GEMV takes (clamped to 1-32). Rows 9 and up need
+`EXL3_GEMV_WMMA` on a gfx12 device and fall back to the cooperative GEMM otherwise. `8` restores
+the original limit, `16` the single-M-tile one, `1` switches the multi-row path off. Read on every
+call.
+
+### `EXL3_GEMV_WMMA_U` (default: `4`)
+
+k-tiles decoded per WMMA batch in the `EXL3_GEMV_WMMA` kernel: `1`, `2` or `4`.
+
 ### `EXL3_INT8_GEMV` (default: `2`)
 
 Fused int8-activation GEMV for tensors quantized with the mul1 codebook: one cooperative launch
@@ -284,6 +380,21 @@ pipeline everywhere, for A/B testing.
 Override the path of the on-disk autotune cache for the cooperative GEMM kernels (kernel shape
 selection results, persisted across runs).
 
+## Speculative decoding
+
+### `EXL3_DRAFT_ROW_BUDGET` (default: `0`, off)
+
+Occupancy-aware draft length. A batched verify forward is (active sequences) x (draft + 1) rows.
+With a budget N, each round drafts `min(num_draft_tokens, N // sequences - 1)` tokens, at least 1,
+so the verify stays within N rows (the multi-row GEMV's fast range: 32 on gfx12 with
+`EXL3_GEMV_WMMA=1`). For example, `16` with 7 draft tokens drafts 7 at one or two jobs and 3 at four.
+The budget is soft: the draft never drops below one token (drafting is what advances AR draft-model
+and MTP caches), so more than N / 2 active sequences verify 2 rows each and exceed N (N = 32 at 17
+sequences: 34 rows). Applies to every draft mode (DFlash2, draft model, MTP, n-gram). `0` or unset
+leaves the draft length alone. Read when the Generator is created. Independently of the budget,
+DFlash2 runs the head and selector over only the block rows a round uses whenever the draft is
+shorter than the block (a short `num_draft_tokens` included).
+
 ## Sampling
 
 ### `EXL3_FUSED_SAMPLER` (default: `1`)
